1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,360
His bank paid better than every other bank. But that was the problem.

2
00:00:05,220 --> 00:00:09,800
Now meet Allen Stanford, a Texan who owned a bank on Antigua, a Caribbean island.

3
00:00:10,380 --> 00:00:15,120
His bank sold CDs, a savings deal: you lock money up, then get a fixed return.

4
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:18,200
But his bank promised more than normal banks were paying.

5
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:23,020
Over about twenty years, it sold more than seven billion dollars of these CDs.

6
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:26,300
That's almost a million dollars every single day.

7
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:28,880
Hit like if that already sounds off to you.

8
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:32,340
So how does a small island bank beat the giant banks?

9
00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:37,460
It didn't. Prosecutors said the returns were inflated, puffed up bigger than real.

10
00:00:38,020 --> 00:00:42,020
Here's the trick. A new customer's money pays an older customer who wants out.

11
00:00:42,540 --> 00:00:45,740
It's actually like paying back one friend with cash borrowed from the next.

12
00:00:46,340 --> 00:00:49,140
Send this to the friend who never trusts a deal that's too good.

13
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:56,560
Then in two thousand nine, the SEC, the government's investing watchdog, sued him, calling it a massive ongoing fraud.

14
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:01,880
In two thousand twelve, a Houston jury found him guilty on thirteen of fourteen counts.

15
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:04,580
Follow if you love when the math catches up.

16
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:09,920
Prosecutors asked for two hundred thirty years. His defense asked for about forty-one months.

17
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:13,540
The judge sentenced him to a hundred and ten years in prison.

18
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:18,540
Plus a five point nine billion dollar money judgment, a court order saying he owes it.

19
00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:23,600
So the bank that beat every other bank was actually paying with the next customer's money.

20
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:27,260
A hundred and ten years. Fair, or too soft?

